    </section>

    <!-- 4 PERMANENT VS INDUCED -->
    <section class="screen" data-teach="permanentinduced">
      <span class="tag lo">Permanent vs induced</span>
      <h2>Two kinds of magnet</h2>
      <p>AQA wants you to tell these two apart precisely:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Permanent magnet</b> — produces its <b>own</b> magnetic field all the time. It can attract <b>or</b> repel (it has fixed poles).</li>
        <li><b>Induced magnet</b> — a magnetic material that becomes a magnet <b>only when placed in a magnetic field</b>. It <b>loses most of its magnetism</b> when removed from the field. An induced magnet <b>always attracts</b> — never repels.</li>
      </ul>
      <figure>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 560 130" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="A permanent magnet induces magnetism in a nearby iron nail">
          <rect x="40" y="48" width="60" height="34" fill="#ff5b6e" stroke="#2c2840" stroke-width="2.5"/><text x="70" y="71" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="15" font-weight="700" fill="#fff">N</text>
          <rect x="100" y="48" width="60" height="34" fill="#2e8bff" stroke="#2c2840" stroke-width="2.5"/><text x="130" y="71" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="15" font-weight="700" fill="#fff">S</text>
          <text x="100" y="40" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="11" font-weight="600" fill="#c8203a">permanent magnet</text>
          <rect x="175" y="52" width="120" height="26" rx="5" fill="#cfd6e0" stroke="#2c2840" stroke-width="2.5"/>
          <text x="190" y="70" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="13" font-weight="700" fill="#0b5bbf">N</text>
          <text x="278" y="70" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="13" font-weight="700" fill="#c8203a">S</text>
          <text x="235" y="100" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="11" font-weight="600" fill="#137a45">iron nail (induced)</text>
          <line x1="160" y1="65" x2="173" y2="65" stroke="#34c777" stroke-width="3"/><polygon points="175,65 165,60 165,70" fill="#34c777"/>
          <text x="360" y="58" font-family="Nunito" font-size="11.5" fill="#6b6580">The nail becomes a magnet</text>
          <text x="360" y="74" font-family="Nunito" font-size="11.5" fill="#6b6580">and is attracted — but loses</text>
          <text x="360" y="90" font-family="Nunito" font-size="11.5" fill="#6b6580">it when the magnet leaves.</text>
        </svg>
      </figure>
      <p class="note"><b>Common mistake:</b> an <b>induced</b> magnet <b>loses most of its magnetism the moment it leaves the field</b> — unlike a permanent magnet, which keeps its own field. And if two objects can <b>repel</b>, both must be permanent magnets: an induced magnet can only ever be <b>attracted</b>.</p>
    </section>

