    <!-- 20 NATIONAL GRID -->
    <section class="screen" data-teach="grid">
      <span class="tag lo">Physics only · National Grid</span>
      <h2>The National Grid</h2>
      <p>The grid carries electricity from power stations to homes. It uses transformers to keep transmission efficient:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Step-up</b> transformer at the power station → very <b>high voltage</b>, which means a <b>low current</b> in the cables.</li>
        <li>Low current means <b>less energy wasted as heat</b> in the transmission cables (heating loss depends strongly on current).</li>
        <li><b>Step-down</b> transformers near towns bring the voltage back down to a <b>safe</b> level (230 V) for use in homes.</li>
      </ul>
      <figure>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 560 130" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="The National Grid steps voltage up for transmission then down for homes">
          <rect x="14" y="50" width="78" height="44" rx="8" fill="#d6f6e4" stroke="#2c2840" stroke-width="2.5"/><text x="53" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="11" font-weight="600" fill="#137a45">power</text><text x="53" y="85" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="11" font-weight="600" fill="#137a45">station</text>
          <polygon points="118,72 138,60 138,84" fill="#ff9f1c" stroke="#2c2840" stroke-width="2"/><text x="128" y="48" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" fill="#9a5a00">step-up</text>
          <line x1="138" y1="72" x2="300" y2="72" stroke="#2c2840" stroke-width="3"/>
          <text x="219" y="62" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="11" font-weight="600" fill="#c8203a">HIGH V, LOW current</text>
          <text x="219" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Nunito" font-size="10.5" fill="#6b6580">→ little heat wasted</text>
          <polygon points="320,60 300,72 320,84" fill="#2e8bff" stroke="#2c2840" stroke-width="2"/><text x="320" y="48" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" fill="#0b5bbf">step-down</text>
          <line x1="320" y1="72" x2="440" y2="72" stroke="#2c2840" stroke-width="3"/>
          <rect x="440" y="50" width="78" height="44" rx="8" fill="#d9ebff" stroke="#2c2840" stroke-width="2.5"/><text x="479" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="11" font-weight="600" fill="#0b5bbf">homes</text><text x="479" y="85" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="11" font-weight="600" fill="#0b5bbf">230 V (safe)</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>Step <b>up</b> for transmission (low current = low heat loss), step <b>down</b> for safe use.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p class="note"><b>Think of it as:</b> sending energy at <b>high voltage</b> is like pushing water through a <b>wide, low-friction pipe</b> — you can move the same amount with only a trickle of <b>current</b>, so very little is wasted heating the cables on the long journey. Near towns the voltage is stepped back <b>down</b> to a safe 230 V.</p>
    </section>

